%% examples/flowchart-shapes.srn — all fourteen bracket spellings Mermaid has
%% for a flowchart node, each drawn as the figure it names. Thirteen of them
%% were refused until this board; the fourteenth, `A[label]`, is the one Siren
%% has always drawn.

%% A file of its own rather than more lines in examples/flowchart-styling.srn
%% or examples/flowchart-syntax.srn, on the precedent those two set: each
%% example keeps one story. Those are about what a document can *do* (every
%% author-styling directive) and how it may be *written* (the statement
%% spellings); this one is about what a node *is*. Folding it into the styling
%% example would also have moved the edge indices its `linkStyle` statements
%% address, which is a trap rather than a demonstration.

%% The shapes are not a gallery: each one is used for what a flowchart has
%% always used it for, which is the only way to see why a diagram wants
%% fourteen of them. Terminal and terminator, input and output, preparation,
%% decision, process, database, predefined process, manual operation and
%% manual input, the on-page connector, a round node on the retry path, and
%% the annotation flag.

%% Measured, not remembered. Everything above the `timeline:` block is valid
%% Mermaid — the block itself is Siren's own syntax, so it is not part of the
%% comparison — and this prints what mermaid 11.17.2 reads out of it:
%%     sed '/^timeline:/,$d' examples/flowchart-shapes.srn \
%%       | node packages/core/scripts/mermaid-probe.mjs -
%% Fourteen vertices, fourteen types, no two the same. `index.test.ts` asserts
%% Siren draws that same set of figures, with zero diagnostics.

%% A shape's *kind* is the contract; its proportions are the theme's. Mermaid
%% draws a wider diamond and a taller cylinder than these; that is not a
%% compatibility gap, it is ADR-0004's line applied to geometry.
flowchart LR

%% `A([text])` — a stadium, the terminal a flowchart starts and ends at. Its
%% ends are semicircles or it is not a stadium, so its corner radius is the
%% renderer's rather than `--siren-node-border-radius`'s: a theme that could
%% flatten it could flatten it out of existence.
Start([Start]) --> Intake[/Read request/]

%% `A[/text/]` — a parallelogram leaning right, which is input, and it pairs
%% with `A[\text\]` further down, which is output. Two spellings that are
%% mirror images, and mixing them up is the commonest way to draw this wrong.
Intake --> Prep{{Prepare}}

%% `A{{text}}` — a hexagon, the preparation step. Both braces are syntax; a
%% reader that took only the outer pair would draw a diamond labelled
%% `{Prepare}`.
Prep --> Check{Valid?}

%% `A{text}` — the decision diamond, and the shape most of what people mean by
%% "a flowchart" is made of. Its label sits *inside* the diamond rather than
%% inside the box the diamond is inscribed in, which is why a shape is not a
%% renderer-only change.
Check --> Parse[Parse]

%% `A[text]` — the plain rectangle, a process. One of only two shapes whose
%% corners are still `--siren-node-border-radius`'s to set; the subroutine
%% below is the other, and the remaining twelve are out of that token's reach.
Parse --> Store[(Store)]

%% `A[(text)]` — a cylinder, the database. Drawn as a `<path>` and not as an
%% `<ellipse>`, deliberately: `rx` is a CSS geometry property on an ellipse,
%% so the theme's `rx: var(--siren-node-border-radius)` would have flattened
%% the lid — and the lid is the figure, not a decoration of it.
Store --> Render[[Render]]

%% `A[[text]]` — a subroutine box, a predefined process called elsewhere. Its
%% two inner bars are the shape; without them it is a rectangle, whatever
%% `data-siren-shape` says.
Render --> Log[\Write log\]

%% `A[\text\]` — a parallelogram leaning the other way: output, the mirror of
%% `Intake` above.
Log --> Done(((End)))

%% `A(((text)))` — a double circle, the terminator. Three pairs of parentheses,
%% and the second ring is the whole difference between this and `A((text))`.

%% The branch a decision earns. `A[/text\]` is a trapezoid — a manual
%% operation — and `A[\text/]` is its inversion, manual input, so the two
%% leaning characters lean the way the shape's own sides do.
Check --> Review[/Approve\]
Review --> Entry[\Operator/]

%% `A((text))` — a circle, the on-page connector: the thing a flowchart uses
%% to rejoin a branch without drawing an edge across the page.
Entry --> Hop((1))
Hop --> Parse

%% `A(text)` — a round node. Rounder than a rectangle and less round than a
%% stadium, and like a stadium it owns its corners rather than borrowing the
%% theme's, so that no token can collapse the four rect-drawn shapes into one.
Check --> Retry(Retry)

%% `A>text]` — the asymmetric flag: the one spelling with no opening bracket
%% at all, and the notch it draws into its left edge is the `>` of the
%% spelling drawn.
Retry --> Flag>legacy path]
Flag --> Prep

%% Every one of the fourteen is named here, so the whole diagram is waiting at
%% step 0 and each shape arrives with the edge that reaches it. A `<path>`, a
%% `<circle>` and a `<rect>` frame all animate the same way: the id and the
%% animation classes sit on the enclosing `<g>`, so the controller drives
%% fourteen shapes without knowing that shapes exist.
timeline:
  enter Start fade, enter Intake fade, enter Start-Intake fade
  enter Prep fade, enter Intake-Prep fade
  enter Check fade, enter Prep-Check fade
  enter Parse fade, enter Check-Parse fade
  enter Review slide-bottom, enter Check-Review fade, enter Entry slide-bottom, enter Review-Entry fade
  enter Hop fade, enter Entry-Hop fade, enter Hop-Parse fade
  enter Retry slide-top, enter Check-Retry fade, enter Flag slide-top, enter Retry-Flag fade, enter Flag-Prep fade
  enter Store fade, enter Parse-Store fade, enter Render fade, enter Store-Render fade
  enter Log fade, enter Render-Log fade, enter Done fade, enter Log-Done fade
